#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111491} :activity/id #uuid "f8c81811-3f72-5396-b8eb-321dbcd75f82" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049053 :topic/id #uuid "d5efe33b-5861-5f40-bd4b-3f871de6b84d" :topic/math-academy-id 1482} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111879 :tutorial/content "We can use logarithms to solve equations where the variable is in an exponent.\nFor instance, suppose we are given the equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that the base of the exponential is ${\\color{blue}2}.$ So, if we take $\\log_{\\color{blue}2}$ of both sides of the equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $\\log_{\\color{blue}b} \\left( {\\color{blue}b}^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\log_{\\color{blue}2} (5).\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form.\n\nNote that when taking the $\\log_b$ of both sides of an exponential equation, we always use the base of the exponential as the base of the logarithm." :tutorial/id #uuid "bcae8943-0768-5280-ad4d-a67f04877fbe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2794 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e9f045cc-a10e-5c66-b731-eb57c6883345" :step/math-academy-id 14453 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110593 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110494 :question/id #uuid "7c937587-dc81-5ee9-aa66-a509a87433e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4067"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f4b65e-b93a-51e8-b943-b3b53d47fffb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544786} {:d